  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Systems · a long essay you can play with",
  "hero.title": "How Chips Share Memory",
  "hero.sub": "The CPU, GPU, accelerator and memory-expansion card in one server each have their own memory.<br>Why is everyone now trying to make them “share one”?",
  "hero.lead": "Two more questions follow. How is a cache kept coherent across a single PCIe wire? And how are the dozens or hundreds of GPUs that train a large model joined into “one machine”? The essay starts with the plainest answer: copy the whole buffer across. Then it shows how CXL adds two new protocols to the PCIe wire, so that a device can cache host memory and the host can read and write device memory; how much slower a memory-expansion card is, and how to use one; how several machines share one pool of memory, and who tells whom when they share the same line; then, on the GPU side, how NVLink and NVSwitch join 72 cards into one domain, how gradients are added up across them, and how domains connect to each other; and last, the practice of packaging a CPU and a GPU together. <strong>Each model is the real mechanism at a smaller scale</strong>: Little's law, the slot accounting of CXL flits, the names of the protocol messages, and Patarasuk and Yuan's ring all-reduce. Each can be checked against the paper or the specification.",
  "hero.note": "This page was written by an AI. The CXL protocol details and latency estimates come from the survey by CXL designer Das Sharma and co-authors; the measured latency of memory-expansion cards comes from Liu et al.'s 2024 measurements; the memory pool comes from Microsoft's Pond paper; and the NVLink, Grace Hopper, MI300A and UALink figures come from the vendors' and consortium's published material. Each source and date is given where it is used. <strong>Values marked “tuned parameter” were chosen so the model can be computed, not measured</strong>; the text says how far a conclusion moves when one is doubled. This page explains mechanisms only and <strong>does not judge which vendor's products are worth buying</strong>.",

