  "ch1.title": "Copy across, or share",
  "ch1.law": "When a device needs data in CPU memory, the old way is to copy the whole block across. The new way is to let the device read each line as a CPU core would, only the lines it uses, with caches kept consistent. Which is faster depends on how big the buffer is, and on how much of it is really used.",
  "ch1.p1": "Two people co-write a report. One way is to copy the whole file onto a USB stick and hand it over; the other person edits it and copies it back. The other way is to put the file in a shared folder, where the other person opens only the pages they need. For a small file of which they read a page or two, sharing is easier. For a large file that they must read from start to finish, one copy is quicker, because a copy moves everything in one go, whereas opening the file page by page means a wait for every page.",
  "ch1.p2": "Most GPUs, network cards and accelerators that plug into PCIe today use the first way. The driver has the device start a DMA that moves a buffer from host memory into the device's own memory (<a href='../how-pcie-works/#ch7'>Chapter 7 of “Why PCIe Keeps Doubling”</a>), and the device copies the results back when it has finished. Each copy has a fixed cost: calling the driver, writing the descriptor, waiting for the completion interrupt. The model takes {dma} µs (<strong>tuned parameter</strong>), then moves bytes at {pcie16} GB/s per direction for PCIe 5.0 ×16 (figure 1, left).",
  "ch1.p3": "The second way needs the device to issue ordinary read requests straight to host memory, one 64-byte line at a time, and the value it reads must always be the latest, even when that line is sitting in some CPU core's cache. The CXL.cache protocol does exactly this (chapter 3). How long does each round trip take? The CXL designers estimate about {cLat} ns. Waiting for one line at a time is too slow, so the device keeps several requests in flight at once. The bandwidth reached is the number of requests in flight times the bytes per request, divided by the round-trip time. This is Little's law (<a href='../why-compute-waits-for-memory/#ch4'>Chapter 4 of “Why Compute Waits for Memory”</a>). With {mlp} requests in flight, it comes to {little64} GB/s (figure 1, right).",
  "ch1.model.title": "Model 1 · Copy a buffer, or read only the lines used",
  "ch1.model.how": "Drag the <strong>buffer size</strong>, choose how much of it the device really uses, and how many requests it can have in flight. Watch where the two lines cross: to the left of the crossing, sharing is faster; to the right, copying is.",
  "ch1.model.aria": "Two curves: the horizontal axis is buffer size (log), the vertical axis is the time for the device to get its data (log). One curve is copying the whole block; the other is reading only the lines used.",
  "ch1.ctl.size": "Buffer size",
  "ch1.ctl.frac": "Share used by the device",
  "ch1.ctl.mlp": "Requests in flight",
  "ch1.frac.1": "All",
  "ch1.frac.0.25": "A quarter",
  "ch1.frac.0.05": "5%",
  "ch1.frac.0.01": "1%",
  "ch1.read.copy": "Whole-block copy (fixed cost + bytes ÷ bandwidth)",
  "ch1.read.share": "Only the lines used (one round trip + lines × 64 bytes ÷ bandwidth)",
  "ch1.read.bw": "Effective bandwidth when sharing",
  "ch1.read.x": "Size at which the two methods tie",
  "ch1.cv.x": "Buffer size",
  "ch1.cv.y": "Time (µs)",
  "ch1.leg.copy": "Whole-block copy",
  "ch1.leg.share": "Only the lines used",
  "ch1.say.share": "A {S} buffer, of which {f} is used: reading only the lines used takes <em>{tShare}</em>, faster than copying ({tCopy}). With {M} requests in flight, the effective bandwidth is {bw}, {bound}. At this share, copying only becomes faster once the buffer exceeds {x}.",
  "ch1.say.copy": "A {S} buffer, of which {f} is used: a whole-block copy takes <em>{tCopy}</em>, faster than reading line by line ({tShare}). The fixed cost of copying is already spread out, and the copy's bandwidth of {bdma} is higher than the {bw} of sharing. Only buffers smaller than {x} are faster to share.",
  "ch1.say.alwaysShare": "With only {f} used, reading just the lines used is faster however big the buffer: now {S} takes <em>{tShare}</em>, and copying takes {tCopy}. Because so few bytes need to move, sharing's lower bandwidth ({bw}) still keeps pace with a whole-block copy.",
  "ch1.bound.little": "limited by Little's law (too few requests)",
  "ch1.bound.link": "limited by the link's own bandwidth",
  "ch1.p4": "The default is a 1 MiB buffer with 5% used by the device: a whole-block copy takes {c1copy} µs, while reading only the lines used takes {c1share} µs. But if the device reads from start to finish, sharing takes {c1fshare} µs and copying wins. With everything used, the two lines cross at {x100} KiB: below that, the fixed cost of copying dominates and sharing wins; above it, the copy's single large burst of bandwidth wins. When the share used is below {fStar} (sharing's effective bandwidth divided by the copy bandwidth), the two lines never cross, and sharing wins at every buffer size. Raise the requests in flight to 256, and sharing is no longer limited by Little's law but by the link itself: the crossing moves to {x100m256} MiB, and the threshold rises to {fStar256}.",
  "ch1.p5": "This is the appeal of sharing one memory: the device can follow a pointer into a data structure in host memory and touch only the part it uses, with no copy first and no need to track which copy has been changed, and by whom. The approach is usually called <strong>zero-copy</strong> or <strong>coherent shared memory</strong>. CXL does it over a PCIe wire; NVIDIA's Grace Hopper and AMD's MI300A do it inside one package (chapter 11). The cost: someone has to make sure every cached copy of a line matches, and that someone is now on the far side of a wire.",
  "ch1.limit": "This is a very rough model. The fixed copy cost of {dma} µs is tuned: halved, the crossing with everything used moves to {x100h} KiB; doubled, to {x100d} KiB; the shape of the result does not change. It leaves out copying the result back, pinning the pages before the copy, shared reads pulling the host's cache along with them, and other traffic on the link. The {cLat} ns is an estimate for the specification's target; chapter 5 shows that the first real devices are slower.",

  "dg.copy.aria": "Diagram: on the left, copy. A buffer in host memory is moved by DMA over PCIe, as one block, into device memory. On the right, share: the device reads the few lines it uses directly from host memory over CXL, and the host keeps them coherent.",
  "dg.copy.cap": "Figure 1. Copy and share (schematic, not to scale). Copy: the whole block is moved across, the device works on its own copy, and keeping the two in step is left to software. Share: the device reads only the lines it uses, and hardware keeps them coherent.",
