  "ch2.title": "Coherence across one wire",
  "ch2.law": "Inside one chip, the cores' caches are kept consistent by one protocol. A device that joins over a wire becomes one more cache in that protocol. CXL is asymmetric: the host decides, and the device only answers.",
  "ch2.p1": "First, a paragraph recapping coherence itself (for the detail, see <a href='../how-cores-share-memory/#ch1'>“How Cores Share Memory”</a>). Each core has its own cache, and several of them can hold copies of the same line at once. The rule: at any moment, either only one cache may write the line, or several may hold it for reading only; a read always returns the last value written. There are two ways to enforce it: everyone listens to every request on the bus (snooping, <a href='../how-cores-share-memory/#ch3'>Chapter 3</a>), or each line has a home that records who holds it (a directory, <a href='../how-cores-share-memory/#ch4'>Chapter 4</a>). The state of each line is one of the letters of MESI (<a href='../how-cpu-caches-work/#ch9'>Chapter 9 of “How a CPU's Three Levels of Cache Work”</a>).",
  "ch2.p2": "Once a device is attached over PCIe, each line of host memory is still managed by the <strong>home agent</strong> in the host. The device's cache becomes one more cache that the home agent looks after. When the device wants to read or write a line, it asks the home first; when a core on the host is about to write a line that the device holds, the home tells the device to invalidate it (figure 2). So that it need not ask the device every time, the host keeps a table of which lines the device may be caching. The size of this table sets how much host memory the device can cache at most; the size of the device's own cache does not matter.",
  "ch2.p3": "How much time does it take to cross a wire? The CXL designers measured on Intel Sapphire Rapids: data from the pins, through the CXL layers and back to the pins, takes {portLo} to {portHi} ns for a round trip, once at each end. The specification sets itself a target: from the host pins, one access to device memory takes no more than {pinMem} ns, and answering one snoop takes no more than {pinSnoop} ns. Swapping a line between cores on one chip takes tens of nanoseconds (<a href='../how-cores-share-memory/#ch7'>Chapter 7 of “How Cores Share Memory”</a>).",
  "ch2.p4": "Why is CXL asymmetric? The links between CPUs (Intel's UPI and AMD's Infinity Fabric, <a href='../how-a-computer-is-wired/#ch4'>Chapter 4 of “How the Parts of a Computer Are Wired Together”</a>) are symmetric: each end holds a full home agent, and the protocols differ between vendors and do not interoperate. CXL aims to let any vendor's device plug into any vendor's CPU, so it keeps the complicated parts in the host: the device need not know the host's internal protocol, only how to answer a few standard requests. In CXL 3.0, one root port can carry up to {cacheIdN} devices with caches (the messages carry a {cacheIdBits}-bit identifier).",
  "ch2.limit": "This chapter has no model. The step-by-step working of the coherence protocol is in the interactive version in “How Cores Share Memory”, and chapter 4 walks through the same steps on CXL messages. The latency figures are measurements on a CXL 1.1 implementation and targets from the specification; they are not a guarantee for any product.",

  "dg.ha.aria": "Diagram: the host has several cores, a last-level cache, a home agent and a table that records which lines the device caches, with host memory (DDR) below. A CXL link connects to the device, which has its own cache, accelerator units and device memory.",
  "dg.ha.cap": "Figure 2. A device's cache joins the host's coherence (schematic). The home agent manages each line of host memory. When the device reads or writes, it asks the home first (solid lines). When a host core is about to write a line the device holds, the home tells the device to invalidate it (dashed lines).",
