  "ch3.title": "Three protocols on one PCIe wire",
  "ch3.law": "CXL lays no new wire. It borrows PCIe's physical layer and sends three protocols in turn, packed into fixed-size frames: one is PCIe itself, and the other two carry cache and memory traffic.",
  "ch3.p1": "Think of a motorway with three kinds of vehicle: coaches, lorries and ambulances. They are all loaded into containers of the same size, and they take turns on the road. It is the same road; only the toll booths can read the labels on the containers and know which department each one must go to.",
  "ch3.p2": "The three kinds are as follows (figure 3). <strong>CXL.io</strong> is the transaction layer of PCIe itself: enumeration, configuration, DMA and interrupts, using PCIe's TLP packets (<a href='../how-pcie-works/#ch3'>Chapter 3 of “Why PCIe Keeps Doubling”</a>); every CXL device must support it. <strong>CXL.cache</strong> lets a device cache host memory: the device sends requests, the host returns data, and the host can also snoop the device. <strong>CXL.mem</strong> works the other way: it lets the host read and write memory on the device as if it were its own memory modules. When the link trains, the two ends first agree, in PCIe's way, that they speak CXL; after that the physical layer carries CXL frames.",
  "ch3.p3": "The CXL 1.1 and 2.0 frame is {flitB} bytes: {flitPid} bytes of protocol ID, saying which protocol the frame carries; {flitPay} bytes of content; and {flitCrc} bytes of CRC check. The content is cut into 4 “slots” of 16 bytes each: request and response headers take up slots, and a 64-byte line of data fills 4 slots. At 64 GT/s, CXL 3.0 switches to PCIe 6.0's 256-byte frame (<a href='../how-pcie-works/#ch10'>Chapter 10 of the same essay</a>), with 16 slots per frame. The cache and memory protocols share one frame format because both need low latency; CXL.io is a separate set, which alternates on the link with the cache and memory set under an arbiter.",
  "ch3.model.title": "Model 2 · How many bytes a CXL link can really move",
  "ch3.model.how": "Choose the <strong>traffic</strong>, the <strong>frame format</strong> and the <strong>number of lanes</strong>. The strip above shows what each slot in a run of frames carries: gold is data, cyan is header, grey is the frame's own overhead. The numbers below are worked out from the slot accounting of Das Sharma et al.",
  "ch3.model.aria": "A run of CXL data frames: each slot is coloured by whether it carries data, a header or overhead, and the colours change with the traffic and frame format chosen.",
  "ch3.ctl.kind": "Traffic",
  "ch3.ctl.mode": "Frame format",
  "ch3.ctl.w": "Lanes",
  "ch3.kind.memRd": "Host reads device memory (CXL.mem)",
  "ch3.kind.cacheRd": "Device reads host memory (CXL.cache)",
  "ch3.kind.cacheWr": "Device writes host memory (CXL.cache)",
  "ch3.mode.68": "68-byte frame · 32 GT/s",
  "ch3.mode.256": "256-byte frame · 64 GT/s",
  "ch3.read.raw": "Raw line rate (per direction)",
  "ch3.read.real": "Data it can really move (per direction)",
  "ch3.read.eff": "Efficiency",
  "ch3.read.ddr": "Equivalent to this many DDR5-4800 channels",
  "ch3.cv.data": "Data",
  "ch3.cv.hdr": "Header",
  "ch3.cv.oh": "Overhead",
  "ch3.say.read": "Of every {slots} slots, {data} carry data; with the efficiency of the frame and the link, {le}, applied, the real rate is <em>{eff}</em> of line speed: {real}, roughly {ddr} DDR5-4800 channels.",
  "ch3.say.write": "Writes cost more: when a device writes a line it must first take ownership, then send the dirty data back. Three message headers take two slots, so only {data} of {slots} slots carry data; the real rate is <em>{eff}</em> of line speed: {real}.",
  "ch3.say.derived": "The survey gives no figure for this cell. Here the 68-byte frame's ratio of two data slots to one header slot is used, multiplied by the 256-byte frame's efficiency of 15/16, giving <em>{real}</em>. This is this page's own calculation, not a figure from the paper.",
  "ch3.p4": "A ×16 link at 32 GT/s has a raw rate of {raw16} GB/s per direction. When the host reads device memory it can really move {fm16} GB/s; device reads of host memory reach {fc16} GB/s; device writes of host memory manage only {fw16} GB/s, because each line written needs three headers. A ×8 memory-expansion card reads at {fm8} GB/s, and one DDR5-4800 channel gives {ddr5} GB/s, so a ×16 link is roughly as much as {fmDdr} memory channels, with far fewer pins (on the memory-module side, see the series essay “How Memory Talks to the CPU”). At 256-byte frames and 64 GT/s, device reads reach {fc256} GB/s and writes {fw256} GB/s.",
  "ch3.limit": "These are the ceilings under full load, with only one kind of traffic and CXL.io not using the line, from the slot accounting in the survey. The measured devices in chapter 5 fall well short of them. The 256-byte figure for host reads of device memory is this page's own calculation, and the model marks it as such.",

  "dg.stack.aria": "Diagram: three protocols, CXL.io, CXL.cache and CXL.mem, each with its transaction and link layers, pass through an arbiter and multiplexer down to PCIe's physical layer. Below, a 68-byte frame: a 2-byte protocol ID, four 16-byte slots and a 2-byte CRC.",
  "dg.stack.cap": "Figure 3. The CXL protocol stack and the 68-byte frame (schematic). CXL.io has its own set; the cache and memory protocols share one. The arbiter alternates the two sets on the same PCIe physical layer. The byte counts come from section 3.2 of Das Sharma et al.'s survey.",
  "dg.stack.io": "CXL.io",
