  "ch4.title": "Three device types, and what each one speaks",
  "ch4.law": "A device speaks only the protocols it needs: one that only caches host memory speaks io and cache; one with its own memory that also caches host memory speaks all three; one that is itself a memory speaks io and mem.",
  "ch4.p1": "The CXL specification divides devices into three types by need (figure 4). <strong>Type 1</strong> has no memory of its own but wants to cache host memory, as a smart NIC does: to handle a packet it reads queues and descriptors in host memory. <strong>Type 2</strong> is an accelerator with its own memory, such as a GPU or an FPGA: it must cache host memory and also open its own memory to the host. <strong>Type 3</strong> is a memory-expansion card: a controller plus some memory, which the host uses as extra memory. It does no computing itself, so it need not cache anyone else's data.",
  "ch4.p2": "Device memory that is mapped into the host's address space, with coherence managed by the host, is called <strong>HDM</strong> (host-managed device memory). The trouble with a Type 2 device is that its memory may also be cached by the host, so when the device changes a line it must first have the host invalidate its copy. Before CXL 3.0, each block of memory had a “bias”, and switching the bias handed over the whole block at once. CXL 3.0 changed this to <strong>back-invalidate</strong>: the device sends an invalidate request straight to the host, and the host, once it has invalidated, replies with a confirmation. The specification calls this “enhanced coherency”.",
  "ch4.model.title": "Model 3 · Two lines, and which messages each device type sends",
  "ch4.model.how": "Choose the <strong>device type</strong>, then press the action buttons. Line X is in host memory; line Y is in device memory. After each step, see the state of each line in each cache, and the messages that crossed the link. Buttons go grey when that device type cannot do the action.",
