  "ch4.dir.d2h": "Device → host",
  "ch4.say.start": "Neither line is cached by anyone yet. Press “Device reads X” to see how the device asks the host for a line.",
  "ch4.say.no": "“{act}” is not possible for this device type: it does not speak {need}.",
  "ch4.say.quiet": "“{act}” sent no messages over the link: either the line was already held locally, or the other side had no copy, so the change is local.",
  "ch4.say.msgs": "“{act}” sent <em>{n} messages</em> over the link. The host always decides: when a device wants a line, it must first ask the host, and the host deals with the line on its side before handing it over.",
  "ch4.say.snoop": "“{act}”: the device holds this line, so the host first snoops it, which took <em>{n} messages</em>. The device's copy {gone}.",
  "ch4.say.bi": "“{act}”: the host caches this line in device memory, so the device must first send a back-invalidate and wait for the host to hand the line back or invalidate its copy: <em>{n} messages</em> in all. This is new in CXL 3.0.",
  "ch4.gone.inval": "was invalidated",
  "ch4.gone.shared": "was downgraded to shared",
  "ch4.p3": "A few presses show CXL's character. When a device reads a line it does not have, it first sends <code>D2H Req RdShared</code>, and the host replies “go ahead” with the data attached. When a CPU wants to write a line the device holds, the host sends <code>H2D Req SnpInv</code> to invalidate the device's copy. A device never deals with the CPU's cache directly: everything goes through the host. A host read of device memory is simply a request and a response (<code>M2S Req MemRd</code> and <code>S2M DRS MemData</code>), because the host still manages the coherence of that memory. A Type 3 device has no CXL.cache, so the read X and write X buttons are grey.",
  "ch4.limit": "This is a simplified state machine: one CPU cache, no simultaneous requests, no internal snoop step among the host's other cores, and no bias switching from before CXL 3.0. Message names follow the survey's spelling; real flows have more variants.",

  "dg.types.aria": "Diagram: three columns. Type 1 device (cache, no memory of its own, speaks io and cache); Type 2 (cache plus device memory, speaks all three); Type 3 (memory only, speaks io and mem). Each column connects to the host above.",
