  "ch5.title": "Memory-expansion cards: a slower tier of memory",
  "ch5.law": "A Type 3 device is just memory, with a controller and a PCIe wire in between: latency is roughly twice that of local memory, and bandwidth equals one or two memory channels. If cold pages are moved there while hot ones stay local, on average it hardly looks slower at all.",
  "ch5.p1": "A bookshelf on your desk holds the books you use often; the store-room downstairs holds the ones you rarely use. However big the store-room, nobody wants to go downstairs for every word; but if a book you consult once a year is kept there, the store-room costs almost nothing, and the desk has room to spare.",
  "ch5.p2": "Why do servers want memory-expansion cards? A CPU can attach only a limited number of memory channels, and each channel needs around two hundred pins; there is no room for more. Cloud providers have also found that, once the cores are rented out, part of the memory often sits unused (chapter 6). A memory card on a PCIe slot adds capacity and bandwidth, and can use cheaper memory modules, or even an older generation.",
  "ch5.p3": "How much slower is it? Two kinds of figure. <strong>Estimates</strong>: the CXL designers, using the specification's targets, put a CPU read of a directly attached expansion card at about {estDirect} ns; Microsoft's Pond paper estimates it at {pondLo} to {pondHi} ns more than local memory on the same node (they measured the local figure at {pondLocal} ns). <strong>Measurements</strong>: in 2024, Liu et al. measured four CXL 1.1 expansion cards on an Intel server with MLC. Local memory was {locLat} ns and {locBw} GB/s per socket. Three ASIC-based cards took {aLat}, {dLat} and {bLat} ns; two ×8 cards reached {aBw} and {bBw} GB/s; a ×16 card reached {dBw} GB/s. An FPGA-based card took {cLatDev} ns. The first products are a good deal slower than the specification's estimates. Before real devices existed, researchers used another socket's memory to stand in for CXL; comparing against real devices, Sun et al. found that true CXL memory can have up to {sunEmu}% lower latency than this emulation, so conclusions from emulated experiments do not carry over directly.",
  "ch5.p4": "How is this slower memory used? The operating system treats it as a remote memory node with no CPU, and when reclaiming memory it demotes cold pages there instead of swapping them to disk (<a href='../how-virtual-memory-works/#ch12'>Chapter 12 of “How Virtual Memory Works”</a>). Hardware can take over too: the “flat memory mode” of Intel Xeon 6 pairs local memory and CXL memory one to one, and a hot line moves to local memory automatically (chapter 12). The key point is that page heat is usually very uneven: a few pages carry most of the accesses.",
  "ch5.model.title": "Model 4 · How many pages on CXL before the average starts to slow",
  "ch5.model.how": "Drag the <strong>share of pages placed on CXL</strong>, choose how uneven page heat is and which card to use, then switch between “cold pages first” and “random”. Watch the share of accesses that land on CXL, the average latency, and the bandwidth when both tiers are used together.",
  "ch5.model.aria": "The horizontal axis is the share of pages placed on CXL; the vertical axis on the left is average idle latency. One line is cold pages placed first, the other is random placement. A small second chart shows the maximum bandwidth when both tiers are used together.",
