  "ch5.say.none": "All pages local: latency {lat}, bandwidth {bw}. Drag right to see how many pages can move before latency changes.",
  "ch5.say.all": "All pages on the expansion card: every access takes {lat}, and bandwidth is only this card's {bw}. Nobody would use it that way.",
  "ch5.say.cheap": "{p} of the pages are on CXL, but only <em>{q}</em> of the accesses land there: average latency is {lat}, only {slow} slower than all-local. Cold pages are hardly accessed, so where they sit makes little difference.",
  "ch5.say.costly": "{p} of the pages are on CXL, and {q} of the accesses land there: average latency is <em>{lat}</em>, {slow} slower than all-local. {why}",
  "ch5.why.hot": "Hot pages are now being pushed over too.",
  "ch5.why.random": "With random placement, the share of accesses equals the share of pages, so the unevenness of heat does not help.",
  "ch5.p5": "The default heat exponent is {zipfS} (<strong>tuned parameter</strong>), with {pages} pages in all. When the coldest half sits on card D, only {t50q} of the accesses land there, average latency is {t50lat} ns, and {t50slow} slower than all-local. Placing the same half at random sends {t50rq} of accesses there, at {t50rlat} ns. At 80%, the accesses landing on CXL are {t80q}, {t80slow} slower: by then the pages being moved are hot. The coldest {reach5} pages can move with latency rising by only 5%; if the unevenness of heat is halved, only {reach5h} can, and with the exponent at 1.2, {reach5s}.",
  "ch5.p6": "Bandwidth is a separate account. If a program is limited by memory bandwidth, sending some accesses to an expansion card can be faster, because both tiers work at once: with 20% of the pages moved across, {tqq} of accesses go over CXL, and together the two tiers deliver at most {tqbw} GB/s, more than local alone. The best split sends {qBest} of the traffic over CXL, for a total of {bwBest} GB/s. The pool of chapter 6 adds one more switch hop (the designers estimate {estSwitch} ns for one read); choose “pool via switch” to see it: with the coldest half moved across, latency is {tSwSlow} higher.",
  "ch5.limit": "The heat exponent is tuned; real programs' page heat changes over time. The latencies in the model are idle latencies; under load both tiers slow down. A rise in latency does not mean the program slows by the same amount, since out-of-order execution hides part of it. Pond measured performance within 1% to 5% of local-only on real cloud workloads (chapter 6). The bandwidth account assumes the program can split its accesses between the tiers in any ratio.",

