  "ch9.title": "All-reduce: how gradients are added up across dozens of GPUs",
  "ch9.law": "Each step of data-parallel training requires every GPU to add its gradients to everyone else's and end up with a copy of the sum. A ring all-reduce has each GPU send only about twice the gradient data, however many GPUs there are; so the time taken depends on each GPU's bandwidth, not on the number of GPUs.",
  "ch9.p1": "Several people each keep their own ledger, and everyone needs the total. The clumsiest way is to hand everything to one person to add up, whose desk is then buried. A smarter way: each person splits their ledger into sections and they sit in a ring; each person passes one section to the right, and the person on the right adds their own section and passes it on. After a round and a half, every section has been summed and has reached everyone, and each person has sent out about two ledgers' worth.",
  "ch9.p2": "This is the bandwidth-optimal all-reduce that Patarasuk and Yuan proved in 2009 (figure 9). N GPUs each cut their data into N chunks. <strong>First, reduce-scatter</strong>: N − 1 steps, in each of which every GPU sends one chunk to the next, which adds it to its own chunk of the same index; afterwards each GPU holds one chunk that is fully summed. <strong>Then all-gather</strong>: another N − 1 steps pass the summed chunks along the ring. Each GPU sends 2(N − 1) chunks in all, each 1/N of the data. They proved that any all-reduce algorithm must have at least one GPU send this much data.",
  "ch9.model.title": "Model 7 · Step through a ring all-reduce",
  "ch9.model.how": "Choose the <strong>number of GPUs</strong>, then press <strong>Next step</strong>. Each row of the table is a GPU, and each cell is one chunk it holds. Gold cells were just received in this step; cyan cells are fully summed.",
